 @LastExile1989  I went from a Red Devil 6700xt  to  Sapphire Nitro+ 7900xtx on a 34in Predator GS ultrawide 1440p.  With 7800x3d.

My 6700xt is stilll running strong in my second computer.  As owner of a 7900xtx its a upgrade but.... Not really worth it to me. I just. Wanted to have. Beast  PC. i wasn't gonna pay near 2k for 4090 . I scratched tht itch, going forward im not getting top models

I did start messing with AI   on linux ROCm is good when it's works.

@iitzfizz  that's actually a tough decision. It would really depend on the manufacturer. The card is basically the same. The 6750 has a higher frequency. But a well-built 6700xt( like a Prime) can out perform power color 6750xt. 
So basically, it comes down to heat management. I would choose the one with the best cooling option. Not just the largest cooler. I have a Sapphire prime it's a small 2 fan 2 slot card. But I can push it well past what AMD says it's can handle, and it stays between 85° - 90° on the hot spot. 

Sorry, I know that's not a very good answer but it's a difficult decision without more information. I hope it helps.

 @Middleseed  I think the RAM issues are only a real problem for 1440p and up.

I am at 1440p with a 7800XT and I dont hit 10 gb in most games I play. Now granted I havent played the latest and greatest except for alan wake 2 which I dont think ate more than 12gb.

Still. WHY CANT NVIDIA PUT MORE RAM ? Legit its always the same shit. Their gpus are overpriced but cant have RAM. 8gb is a disgrace on anything but a 50 card.

 @aminy23   I know its designed for laptops, but that doesn't mean it has to be this crap. Cause the rx6700m which came on laptops first and then on desktops and had 160bit and 10gb of vram and similar perf to this rx7600. If anything, this rx7600 seems more like an OC'd rx7500xt with the 16gb variant being kinda useless. The rtx 4060ti is a good 20-30% faster for a reason. 

Even when nvidia was on samsung 8nm, they rivalled amd's rdna 2's efficiency despite rdna 2 using a much better tsmc 7nm. Even if nvidia just used TSMC 6nm, they'd still have beaten AMD by a wide margin. It also seems like AMD was expecting rdna 3 to be more efficient than it actually is. 

AMD's navi 33 offerings on laptops are not much more efficient than nvidia's ampere gpu's. They also cost more than nvidia's offerings. Where's the cost savings? Even budget laptops cool 100-150w GPU's, so AMD does not have to worry about TDP. They don't even have the rx7600mxt in any meaningful capacity.

 @aminy23  My point with rx6700m was just because its designed for laptops, does not mean it has to suck in design. Rx6600 was great on desktops and yet on laptops it sucked compared to the rtx 3060 mobile. 

Nvidia should've indeed used a cheaper 6nm node for low to midrange. AMD's just got issues with RDNA 3 which affected its performance and efficiency.

Node nm is now just a name. And samsung 8nm node is significantly worse than TSMC's 7nm node. I'd say about the same difference as TSMC 6nm vs 4nm (4nm is just a refined 5nm) if not greater.

 @Frashsibit  True - at least in US market. Let me check my own market...
OK. Availability is still okay for RX 6700. The problem for AMD is that it costs exactly the same as RX 7600. The 8GB version (though there is bigger variance on 6700xt). Both have an average price of around 310$ (tax included). No data yet on 7600 XT, because it will be available like 2 more days from now. Given the "spread" of prices though - rx 7600 seems more stable in price, so if we factor that in, price of 6700xt rises to 322$

Unless you need codecs (which I think Navi 33 has), there is no way that I would choose 7600 over 6700xt even if it will be on average 12$ cheaper. Personally I bought RX 6700 - but that was over a year ago. Good 10GB card, that is still stronger than 7600 and has that additional 2GB of VRAM. But sadly while it has decent volume left, it was bought by retailers and stockpiled 1,5 year ago. So they sell it at 310$ price. So while this card is still better than 7600, it is only 12$ less than "full" 6700xt.

 @betag24cn  Go back to the 5000 series and AMD's drivers should have been Alpha with the 5700XT blackscreen issues. Today they're barely out of beta. 

AMD caught up a little with gaming, but Nvidia slays outside with everything from streaming to AI largely because of Beta testing. 

Those ok with a Beta might consider an Alpha. Intel Arc is getting better with gaming much the same way AMD did.

 @aminy23  But what are they countering with this card? For most people the 16GB barely have any benefits. 12GB i could understand maybe, but this just looks like they wanted to rival nvidia in their stupidity.
And we have to keep in mind that green cards a re still often far ahead of AMD cards when it comes to professional applications where its probably the most likely that any application could utilize 16GB on a card like this.

I dunno what their gameplan is with this one.

 @GamersNexus  I was wondering about AI use cases to, since bigger VRAM allows for larger batch sizes which is very handy.
However I think ROCm isn't as mature as CUDA. I thought perhaps if AMD was to release a new improved version of ROCm in conjunction with 7600 XT making AI development for personal use more viable on their toolchains, this could be a big win. But I haven't heard anything so far.
If you did manage to dabble on the AI side of these cards in your reviews, I'd be quite interested. I guess many students or small businesses would be as well.
